.TH CATALIST 1 "November 2026" "v@PROJECT_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
catalist, cl \- local-first anime and manga tracker for the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B catalist
\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIOPTION\fR...] [\fIARG\fR...]
.br
.B cl
\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIOPTION\fR...] [\fIARG\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B catalist
tracks the anime you watch and the manga you read in one SQLite file on
your computer, from the shell, in scripts and in pipes.
.B cl
is the same program under a shorter name.
.PP
Only \fBadd\fR and \fBsearch\fR go online, to AniList's public API,
through curl. A title's total, and how many of its episodes were out, are
what AniList said when it was added.
.PP
A \fIREF\fR names a title: its ref, the first column of \fBls\fR
(\fBa:12\fR is an anime, \fBm:3\fR a manga), or part of its title, in
capitals or not. A title that is exactly the words given wins; otherwise
several matches are an error, unless \fB\-\-all\fR takes them all. A lone
\fB\-\fR reads the refs from standard input, one a line, so
\fBcatalist ls \-q\fR can feed another command. The ref of a removed title
can be given to the next title added.
.PP
Options come before the operands, may be grouped (\fB\-amq\fR), and take
their value joined or apart (\fB\-n3\fR, \fB\-n 3\fR,
\fB\-\-sort=title\fR); \fB\-\-\fR ends them. Every command takes
\fB\-h\fR and \fB\-\-help\fR.
.PP
At a terminal, lists are tables. Into a pipe or a file, each title is one
line of fields with a tab between them, and \fB\-\-json\fR prints one JSON
object a line. Messages, questions and errors go to standard error.
.PP
Each command that changes the lists is one step for \fBundo\fR; commands
run with the same \fBCATALIST_TX\fR are one step together (see \fBtx\fR).
\fBredo\fR makes again what \fBundo\fR took back, until the next change.
The last 100 steps are kept.
.SH COMMANDS
@COMMANDS@
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/catalist/catalist.db
The lists, the provider and the steps \fBundo\fR and \fBredo\fR can take,
in one SQLite file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/catalist/anime.csv, manga.csv, settings
The files of Catalist 0.3.0 and earlier. When there is no
\fIcatalist.db\fR yet, they are read into a new one, once, and left as they
were.
.PP
Catalist-TUI, the full-screen interface, keeps its lists in the same
\fIcatalist.db\fR.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CATALIST_DATA_DIR
Keep the lists in this directory. The old files are then looked for in
it, too.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
When set to an absolute path, the lists are kept in
\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/catalist\fR.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the old files of Catalist 0.3.0 and earlier are looked for:
\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/catalist\fR.
.TP
.B CATALIST_TX
A label from \fBcatalist tx\fR. Commands run with the same label make one
step for \fBundo\fR.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set, output has no colours.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when the work failed (no title matched, a file could not
be read, the lists could not be saved, AniList could not be reached), 2
when the command line is wrong or names no command. A pipe closed early, as by \fBhead\fR, is
not a failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
catalist add \-\-pick 1 frieren
catalist + frieren
catalist ls \-q \-s watching | catalist + \-
catalist set a:12 score=9 status=completed
catalist undo
catalist export \-\-format=csv anime > anime.csv
.fi
.SH AUTHORS
McKaigne <https://github.com/McKaigne/Catalist>
